Step 1: What is Shell Scripting?

A Shell Script is a text file containing Linux commands that are executed one after another.

Instead of typing the same commands every day, you write them once in a script and run the script whenever needed.

Without a Script

Every day you manually run:

df -h
free -h
uptime
systemctl status jenkins
With a Script

You run only:

./health-check.sh

The script performs all the commands automatically.

Step 2: Create Your First Script

Create a file:

touch hello.sh

Edit it:

vi hello.sh

Add:

#!/bin/bash

echo "Hello DevOps"
echo "Welcome to Shell Scripting"

Save and exit.

Make it executable:

chmod +x hello.sh

Run it:

./hello.sh

Output:

Hello DevOps
Welcome to Shell Scripting
Step 3: Variables

Variables store values.

Example:

#!/bin/bash

NAME="Sunil"

echo "Welcome $NAME"

Output:

Welcome Sunil
Step 4: User Input

Read input from the keyboard:

#!/bin/bash

echo "Enter your name:"
read NAME

echo "Hello $NAME"

Example:

Enter your name:
Sunil
Hello Sunil
Step 5: Conditional Statements
#!/bin/bash

echo "Enter a number:"
read NUM

if [ "$NUM" -gt 10 ]; then
    echo "Greater than 10"
else
    echo "10 or less"
fi
Step 6: Multiple Conditions
#!/bin/bash

read MARKS

if [ "$MARKS" -ge 90 ]; then
    echo "Grade A"
elif [ "$MARKS" -ge 75 ]; then
    echo "Grade B"
else
    echo "Grade C"
fi
Step 7: for Loop
#!/bin/bash

for i in 1 2 3 4 5
do
    echo "Number: $i"
done

Output:

Number: 1
Number: 2
Number: 3
Number: 4
Number: 5
Step 8: while Loop
#!/bin/bash

COUNT=1

while [ "$COUNT" -le 5 ]
do
    echo "$COUNT"
    COUNT=$((COUNT + 1))
done
Step 9: Functions
#!/bin/bash

greet() {
    echo "Welcome to DevOps"
}

greet
Step 10: Exit Codes

Every Linux command returns an exit status.

Check it:

ls
echo $?
0 → Success
Non-zero → Error

Example:

ls /notfound
echo $?

Output:

2

This is very useful in automation and Jenkins pipelines.

Step 11: Real DevOps Script – Server Health Check

Create health-check.sh:

#!/bin/bash

echo "===== Server Health Report ====="

echo "Hostname:"
hostname

echo
echo "Uptime:"
uptime

echo
echo "Disk Usage:"
df -h

echo
echo "Memory Usage:"
free -h

echo
echo "Top 5 CPU Processes:"
ps -eo pid,comm,%cpu --sort=-%cpu | head -6

echo
echo "Jenkins Status:"
systemctl status jenkins --no-pager

Make it executable:

chmod +x health-check.sh

Run:

./health-check.sh
Step 12: Jenkins Integration

In Jenkins, add a Build Step:

Execute shell

Example:

#!/bin/bash

echo "Starting Build"

mvn clean package

echo "Build Completed"

When the Jenkins job runs, it executes the shell script automatically.

